_input_tokens": 5044}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:05:27.470Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWBQ42ahUheaaR5CqeRH", "thinking_duration_ms": 116464}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWBQ4SPMCReb6eenbYJG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I traced each plan's cash and stock day by day on mean demand. Where the plans differ, the main swing factors are: a cash shortfall on day 4, how far each plan orders above expected demand, the 156-unit capacity limit, and the number of order days (44 each).\n\n**Shared start.** Every plan runs short on days 1–2. The starting stock and the day-0 sales leave roughly 470–900 in cash going into day 4.\n\n**Plans 0–4** order every second day. On day 4 the cash isn't enough, so product 1 gets cut:\n- Plan 0 gets 0 of 78 units.\n- Plan 4 gets about 27 of 63.\n- Plan 2 gets about 21 of 72.\n- Plan 1 gets about 35 of 69.\n- Plan 3 loses only about 6 units.\n\nThat batch arrives on day 8, which costs plans 0, 2 and 4 roughly 350–500 in lost sales and penalties, and plan 1 about 250. On top of that, plan 0 orders about 1.2–1.25× expected demand on both products, and plan 4 does the same on product 0. That leaves hundreds of units to spoil or be left over, and around day 24 plan 0 also overflows the warehouse.\n\n**Plan 1** orders about 1.1× expected demand. **Plan 2** orders about 1.11× and trims its last orders, which cuts end-of-period leftovers. **Plan 3** orders about 1.0× expected demand. That avoids waste, but a shortfall costs 14–16 per unit against roughly 9 for a surplus, so it gives up some shortage cover.\n\n**Plan 5** orders every 3 days, which gives the lowest fixed cost. But on day 22 the warehouse limit forces only 100 units of product 1 against about 145 expected for days 22–24. That costs about 600, more than the roughly 220 it saves in order fees.\n\n**Plan 6** orders daily at about 1.05–1.1× demand and has no cash problems. But it pays about 13 more order fees than the every-second-day plans, roughly 570 extra.\n\n**Plan 7** starts with a small first order, which avoids the day-4 cash crunch: it loses only about 5 units on day 5. It orders about 1.08× demand and keeps its final orders tight. I rate it the strongest.\n\n**Ranking.** Plans 7, 3, 1 and 2 are my picks for the top half. My main uncertainty is plan 2 against plan 6, and to a lesser extent plan 5. Plans 0 and 4 are clearly at the bottom."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5044, "cache_read_input_tokens": 886, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 5044}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:05:37.522Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWBQ42ahUheaaR5CqeRH"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_016FUfh9gjTf4DTuiGGhyyoJ", "name": "StructuredOutput", "input": {}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWBQ4SPMCReb6eenbYJG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant"